WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.720 --> 00:00:04.000
Most parents feel safe letting their&nbsp;
kids accompany them to see the latest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.000 --> 00:00:06.240
Marvel blockbuster, but there's also plenty of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.240 --> 00:00:09.600
MCU content that's meant just for&nbsp;
the grown-ups. With that in mind,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.600 --> 00:00:13.840
here are a few things only adults will notice&nbsp;
in "Shang-Chi and the Legend of the Ten Rings."

00:00:14.720 --> 00:00:19.040
"On a dark desert highway, cool wind in my&nbsp;
hair ..." Those ten words from Katy in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.040 --> 00:00:23.440
scaffolding scene will almost certainly resonate&nbsp;
with every adult in the "Shang-Chi" audience,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.440 --> 00:00:27.600
who can't help but recognize the introductory&nbsp;
line to one of the most popular songs in 20th&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.600 --> 00:00:30.800
century adult-oriented album rock.
Written and performed by the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.800 --> 00:00:35.360
Eagles and released in 1977 on the album&nbsp;
of the same name, "Hotel California" won&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.360 --> 00:00:39.600
the Grammy for Record of the Year and soared to&nbsp;
the number one position on the Billboard charts.&nbsp;

00:00:39.600 --> 00:00:42.320
Of course, kids in the "Shang-Chi"&nbsp;
audience won't know any of this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.320 --> 00:00:46.800
as it seems unlikely they're sleeping under a&nbsp;
Don Henley poster on their bedroom wall. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.800 --> 00:00:51.120
"Hotel California" has become an inescapable&nbsp;
part of the American songbook — you might not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.120 --> 00:00:55.360
be rocking the Eagles on Spotify, but you're no&nbsp;
less destined to hear the song in an elevator, at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.360 --> 00:00:59.280
the bank or ... you know ... featured prominently&nbsp;
in one of the year's biggest blockbuster movies.&nbsp;

00:00:59.280 --> 00:01:02.160
Of course, the song comes around&nbsp;
again later on, when the good guys&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.160 --> 00:01:06.160
have triumphed and Katy and Shaun want to&nbsp;
celebrate with Wong — and some karaoke.&nbsp;

00:01:07.200 --> 00:01:10.800
Like the Eagles' connection, "Shang-Chi and&nbsp;
the Legend of the Ten Rings" is stuffed with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.800 --> 00:01:14.720
references more likely to make mom and&nbsp;
dad laugh than the kids — but what Marvel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.720 --> 00:01:18.480
always does well is write these lines&nbsp;
so everyone can understand the context,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.480 --> 00:01:20.640
regardless of their pop culture prowess.&nbsp;

00:01:20.640 --> 00:01:24.960
Then again, "pop culture" is stretching things&nbsp;
when Katy makes passing reference to Talbots,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.960 --> 00:01:29.840
a 74-year-old American specialty retailer&nbsp;
with more than 400 locations nationwide.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.840 --> 00:01:34.160
The store is best known for being the go-to place&nbsp;
to grab a holiday gift card for your grandmother.&nbsp;

00:01:34.160 --> 00:01:39.040
In another scene, Shaun is called "Gangnam&nbsp;
Style." This attempted slur compared him to Psy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.040 --> 00:01:43.600
the worldwide sensation who in 2012 set a world&nbsp;
record for having the first clip on YouTube to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.600 --> 00:01:49.600
surpass a billion views; as of 2021, the "Gangnam&nbsp;
Style" music video had reached 4 billion views,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.600 --> 00:01:54.000
and was the tenth-most viewed and fifth-most&nbsp;
liked video on YouTube. Shaun explains that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.000 --> 00:01:58.560
this is both insensitive and ignorant, since&nbsp;
he is Chinese and Psy is from South Korea.&nbsp;

00:01:58.560 --> 00:02:02.560
Katy also references Jeff Gordon, one of the&nbsp;
most influential and decorated drivers in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.560 --> 00:02:07.840
history of NASCAR. Currently an announcer for Fox&nbsp;
NASCAR, Gordon is a four-time Cup Series champion,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.840 --> 00:02:11.760
three-time winner of the Daytona 500, and&nbsp;
became the youngest driver to win a NASCAR&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.760 --> 00:02:16.480
title at the age of 24. As it turns out, the&nbsp;
reference is a bit of foreshadowing, as Katy's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.480 --> 00:02:20.800
obvious enthusiasm for racing later manifests&nbsp;
itself in some slick moves behind the wheel of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.800 --> 00:02:24.960
Razor Fist's car while our heroes navigate&nbsp;
the magically-shifting jungle around Ta-Lo.&nbsp;

00:02:25.840 --> 00:02:30.160
To understand the true origins of Shang-Chi,&nbsp;
you have to go back to a time and place:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.160 --> 00:02:34.400
comic books in the early 1970s. When the&nbsp;
character first appeared in "Special Marvel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.400 --> 00:02:40.000
Edition" #15 in 1973, Marvel was dominating the&nbsp;
industry, but saw growth potential elsewhere:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.000 --> 00:02:44.240
riding the martial arts craze kicked off by&nbsp;
Bruce Lee and his huge hit "Enter the Dragon."&nbsp;

00:02:44.240 --> 00:02:47.920
It's easy now to look back on those&nbsp;
movements as condescending, money-grabbing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.920 --> 00:02:52.640
largely-white-financed efforts to co-opt heartfelt&nbsp;
sentiment. It's also undeniable that for the first&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.640 --> 00:02:57.120
time in popular culture, Asian-American audiences&nbsp;
were seeing themselves on screen — not as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.120 --> 00:03:01.600
punchlines and purse-snatchers, but as heroes.
Which isn't to say those Shang-Chi adventures&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.600 --> 00:03:04.960
have aged well. He was originally&nbsp;
presented as the son of Fu Manchu,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.960 --> 00:03:09.440
a character who perpetuated "Yellow Peril"&nbsp;
stereotypes. The early comics often portrayed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.440 --> 00:03:14.400
Shang-Chi as "subservient," kicking butt in battle&nbsp;
but requiring guidance in social situations.&nbsp;

00:03:14.400 --> 00:03:18.000
As a result of these and other issues&nbsp;
in the character's problematic history,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.000 --> 00:03:22.000
writer Dave Callaham has revealed that the&nbsp;
behind-the-scenes team for "Shang-Chi" created&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.000 --> 00:03:26.160
a list of racist stereotypes they were determined&nbsp;
to, quote, "destroy" while scripting the movie.&nbsp;

00:03:26.960 --> 00:03:30.960
In Shaun's modest apartment at the beginning of&nbsp;
the film, he has all the essentials of bachelor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.960 --> 00:03:36.400
life: a bed, a TV, space to do push-ups. He&nbsp;
also has some cool posters adorning his walls,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.400 --> 00:03:39.760
which might allude to themes director&nbsp;
Destin Daniel Cretton was attempting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.760 --> 00:03:43.760
to tap into for his Marvel debut.
One of the posters is for 2004's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.760 --> 00:03:48.240
"Kung Fu Hustle." Written and directed by Stephen&nbsp;
Chow, it's a Chinese action-comedy that follows&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.240 --> 00:03:52.400
an aspiring gangster, a poor village, and&nbsp;
the colorful characters that orbit them.&nbsp;

00:03:52.400 --> 00:03:55.360
Often lumped in with "Crouching&nbsp;
Tiger, Hidden Dragon" and "Hero"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.360 --> 00:03:59.040
as modern examples of wuxia films that&nbsp;
have caught on with American audiences,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.040 --> 00:04:02.880
the film is nonetheless unique in that it&nbsp;
brandishes a sense of humor not present in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.880 --> 00:04:08.000
the others. As it turns out, it is a tone very&nbsp;
similar to the one struck by "Shang-Chi" as well.&nbsp;

00:04:08.000 --> 00:04:12.800
"A guy with a freakin' machete for&nbsp;
an arm just chopped our bus in half!"&nbsp;

00:04:12.800 --> 00:04:17.360
Oh yeah, it also features a character who&nbsp;
specializes in fighting with iron rings.&nbsp;

00:04:17.360 --> 00:04:21.440
Another poster is perhaps less of an obvious&nbsp;
influence on "Shang-Chi and the Legend of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.440 --> 00:04:26.000
Ten Rings," but no less classic of a film.&nbsp;
It's Walter Hill's "The Warriors," which is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:26.000 --> 00:04:30.800
about a near-futuristic New York ruled by&nbsp;
eccentric gangs. Considered a cult classic,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.800 --> 00:04:35.280
the film contains brutal violence, a gleeful&nbsp;
nihilistic attitude, and style to spare.&nbsp;

00:04:36.400 --> 00:04:40.240
Sure, they look cool in the movie. But&nbsp;
some adults watching may know that iron&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.240 --> 00:04:44.960
rings are a real part of multiple martial arts&nbsp;
disciplines — especially "Kung Fu Hustle" fans.&nbsp;

00:04:44.960 --> 00:04:49.440
Watch any video of a Kung Fu master using iron&nbsp;
rings and you'll get an idea of how they can add&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:49.440 --> 00:04:54.320
resistance to arm movements, hardening muscle and&nbsp;
strengthening the arms and fists of combatants.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.320 --> 00:04:57.920
Warning: they are extremely noisy, so&nbsp;
you might want to leave them at home if&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.920 --> 00:05:01.120
you're hoping to preserve the element&nbsp;
of surprise against your opponent.&nbsp;

00:05:01.120 --> 00:05:05.360
Of course, real life iron rings won't allow&nbsp;
the user to throw fireballs, climb up them like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.360 --> 00:05:09.920
steps or throw them like boomerangs. But if the&nbsp;
non-magical kind were good enough for Bruce Lee,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:09.920 --> 00:05:15.120
they'd probably help with your training as well.
"I actually did take a little bit of martial arts&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.120 --> 00:05:18.320
as a youth, so I'm gonna try and&nbsp;
grade this fight as we're going."&nbsp;

